# Angular Signals — signal(), computed(), effect() and Why effect() Is a Last Resort

## 1. What is it?

A **Signal** is a wrapper around a value that knows *who is reading it* and can automatically notify those readers when the value changes. You read a signal by **calling it like a function** (`count()`), and you change it through explicit methods (`.set()`, `.update()`) — never by direct assignment.

Angular gives you three core building blocks:

- **`signal()`** — a writable, observable "box" holding a value.
- **`computed()`** — a read-only, automatically-derived value calculated from other signals.
- **`effect()`** — a side-effect function that automatically reruns whenever any signal it reads changes.

Together they form Angular's **fine-grained reactivity system**, introduced as developer preview in Angular 16 and stabilized in Angular 17+.

## 2. Why was it introduced?

Before signals, Angular's change detection relied on **Zone.js**: Zone.js monkey-patches async browser APIs (`setTimeout`, `addEventListener`, promises, etc.) so that whenever *any* async event fires anywhere in the app, Angular re-checks the **entire component tree** (or at least every component not behind `OnPush`) to see what changed.

This created real problems at scale:

- It's **coarse-grained** — one button click can trigger a full-tree check even though only one small piece of UI actually changed.
- It's **hard to reason about** — you often can't tell, just by reading a component, *why* it re-rendered or *when* exactly change detection ran.
- Working around it required discipline: `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`, immutable data patterns, and manual `markForCheck()` calls — easy to get subtly wrong, especially in large teams.
- Using RxJS for local component state (e.g. `BehaviorSubject` + `async` pipe) works, but comes with real boilerplate: subscription management, `takeUntil` cleanup, and a mental model (streams over time) that's overkill for "this component just needs to hold and derive some values."
///

The old way — RxJS for local state

Before signals existed, a common pattern for holding simple state inside a component looked like this:

ts
private countSubject = new BehaviorSubject<number>(0);
count$ = this.countSubject.asObservable();

doubled$ = this.count$.pipe(map(c => c * 2));

increment() {
  this.countSubject.next(this.countSubject.value + 1);
}

And in the template you'd use the async pipe:

html
<p>{{ count$ | async }}</p>
<p>{{ doubled$ | async }}</p>

This does work. But the sentence is pointing out three costs of doing it this way:
1. "Subscription management"
Every Observable you subscribe to manually (in .ts code, not via the async pipe) needs to be unsubscribed from eventually, or you leak memory — the subscription keeps a reference alive even after the component is destroyed. So you end up writing boilerplate like storing a Subscription and calling .unsubscribe() yourself.

2. "takeUntil cleanup"
A very common pattern to avoid that manual unsubscribe bookkeeping is:

////////////////////////////////////////
Imagine you walk into a room and turn on a radio. The radio keeps playing music forever, automatically, until someone tells it to stop.

Now imagine you leave the room. If nobody turns the radio off, it just... keeps playing. Forever. In an empty room. Nobody's listening, but it's still using electricity, still running.

That's a subscription in RxJS. When you call .subscribe(...), you've turned on a radio. It keeps running, doing work, until something explicitly tells it to stop.

An Angular component is like that room. When the component is destroyed (user navigates away, closes a tab, etc.), the "room" disappears — but if you started a subscription inside it and never stopped it, that subscription is the radio still playing in a room that no longer exists.

Why that's actually bad, not just wasteful

The radio doesn't just waste electricity — it's still holding a reference to things in that destroyed room. If your subscription callback does something like this.userName = ..., it's still trying to touch a component that's supposed to be gone. The component can't be fully garbage-collected because this leftover subscription is still holding onto it. Do this a hundred times (user navigates back and forth 100 times) and you have 100 ghost radios still playing, still holding memory, maybe even still trying to update a screen that isn't there anymore. That's the "memory leak."

Now the fix, in plain words

You need two things:

A way to say "stop" — some signal you can trigger.
A way to tell each radio "listen for that stop signal, and shut yourself off when you hear it."

That's it. That's the whole pattern. Now let's map that directly onto the code, one line at a time.

ts
private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();

This is just the stop button. It doesn't play music, it doesn't hold data — it's a button that, when pressed, sends out a little "STOP" pulse to anyone listening for it.

ts
ngOnInit() {
  this.someStream$.pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$)).subscribe(...);
}

This turns the radio on (.subscribe), but with a rule attached: "keep playing, but the moment the stop button (destroy$) is pressed, turn yourself off." That rule is what takeUntil(this.destroy$) means. Read it literally: "take values until this thing (destroy$) fires."

ts
ngOnDestroy() {
  this.destroy$.next();
  this.destroy$.complete();
}

ngOnDestroy is Angular automatically telling you: "this room is being torn down right now." So the very first thing you do is press the stop button — this.destroy$.next(). Every radio that was told to listen for this button (every subscription piped through takeUntil(this.destroy$)) hears it and shuts off immediately. .complete() right after is just tidying up the button itself, since it's done its one job.

The timeline, start to finish
Component loads → ngOnInit runs → radio turns on, listening for the stop button.
Radio plays for a while (subscription keeps receiving values, doing its job).
User navigates away → Angular destroys the component → ngOnDestroy fires automatically.
ngOnDestroy presses the stop button (destroy$.next()).
The radio, which was told "listen for that button," hears it and shuts off on its own.
No ghost radio left running. Component can be fully cleaned up.
The one sentence to keep

destroy$ is a button you press exactly once, when the component dies. takeUntil(this.destroy$) is how you tell any subscription "listen for that button, and turn yourself off when it's pressed." That's the entire mental model — everything else is just RxJS syntax for wiring that up.


--Multiple radios, one button

Say your component has three separate subscriptions doing different jobs:

ts
ngOnInit() {
  this.streamA$.pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$)).subscribe(val => this.doThingA(val));
  this.streamB$.pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$)).subscribe(val => this.doThingB(val));
  this.streamC$.pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$)).subscribe(val => this.doThingC(val));
}

Three separate radios, playing three different things. But notice — all three are wired to listen for the same stop button (this.destroy$).

ts
ngOnDestroy() {
  this.destroy$.next();
  this.destroy$.complete();
}

When you press that one button, all three radios hear it at the same instant and shut off together — because .next() on a Subject broadcasts to every single thing listening to it, not just one. You don't need destroy$A, destroy$B, destroy$C. One button, any number of listeners.
/////////////////////////////


3. "A mental model (streams over time) that's overkill"
RxJS's whole paradigm is "everything is a stream of values arriving over time, and you compose/transform that stream." That's genuinely powerful for things like HTTP calls, websockets, or search-as-you-type (the switchMap stuff from earlier). But if all you actually want is "this component has a count number that starts at 0 and I want to increment it and show double it" — that's not really a stream problem. There's no "over time" element worth modeling — it's just a value that changes. Forcing it through Subjects, operators, and the async pipe is using a sophisticated tool for a simple job.

The point of the sentence

It's setting up a contrast (presumably the next part of the answer talks about signals as the alternative). With signals, the same thing is just:

count = signal(0);
doubled = computed(() => this.count() * 2);

increment() {
  this.count.update(c => c + 1);
}
No Subject, no subscription, no takeUntil, no async pipe, no manual cleanup — because signals aren't push-based streams you subscribe to, they're just readable values that Angular tracks automatically. For simple local component state, this is less code and a simpler mental model. RxJS still earns its keep for genuinely asynchronous, event-driven things (HTTP, websockets, debounced search) — the sentence isn't saying "RxJS is bad," it's saying "it's the wrong tool specifically for plain local state."

//
**Problem without signals:** you had two imperfect options — coarse, zone-driven change detection (simple but wasteful), or RxJS for everything (precise but heavy for simple synchronous state).

**What it solves:** Signals track dependencies automatically and precisely. When a signal changes, Angular knows *exactly* which parts of the template actually depend on it and can update just those parts — without needing Zone.js to "poll" the whole tree. This is also the foundation for **zoneless Angular** (removing Zone.js entirely for better performance and smaller bundles), and it gives local component state a much lighter-weight API than reaching for RxJS every time.

## 3. How does it work?

### `signal()`   --> https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-5srzjc1q-8gbmyf3t?file=src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts

```ts
const count = signal(0);   // WritableSignal<number>
count();                   // read: 0
count.set(5);               // write: replace the value entirely
count.update(v => v + 1);   // write: derive the new value from the old one
```

- Read it by **calling it as a function**. This is what lets Angular's reactivity system track "who read this."
- `.set()` replaces the value outright; `.update()` computes the new value from the previous one — always prefer `.update()` when the new value depends on the current one, and always return a **new** object/array reference for objects/arrays (signals compare with `===` by default, so mutating in place won't trigger updates).

### `computed()`

```ts
const doubleCount = computed(() => count() * 2);
```

- Angular automatically tracks which signals were read *during* the computed function's execution — no manual dependency array, no `distinctUntilChanged` boilerplate.
- It's **lazy**: the function doesn't run until something actually reads `doubleCount()` for the first time.
- It's **memoized**: it only recomputes when one of its dependencies (`count` here) actually changes value; reading it repeatedly without a dependency change just returns the cached result.
- It's **read-only** — there's no `.set()` on a computed signal. If you need to write a derived value, that's a sign you should be deriving it with `computed()`, not manually keeping it in sync.

### `effect()`

```ts
effect(() => {
  console.log('count changed to', count());
});
```

- Reruns automatically whenever any signal read inside it changes — same automatic dependency tracking as `computed()`.
- Runs **once immediately** on creation (to establish its dependencies and do its first run), then again on every subsequent change to any signal it reads.
- Must be created in an **injection context** — typically a component/service constructor, or a field initializer, or explicitly passed an `Injector`. You can't just call `effect()` from anywhere.
- Unlike `computed()`, it doesn't return a value — it exists purely to *do something* (log, write to storage, call a non-Angular API), not to produce a piece of derived state.

## 4. Example

**Live, working StackBlitz project:** https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-5srzjc1q

`src/app/app.component.ts`:

```ts
import { Component, signal, computed, effect } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';

interface CartItem {
  name: string;
  price: number;
  qty: number;
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule],
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
})
export class AppComponent {
  // signal(): a writable, observable box holding a value.
  // Read it by CALLING it: count() -- not count.
  count = signal(0);

  // computed(): a read-only DERIVED signal. Angular tracks that this
  // function reads count(), so it automatically re-runs (and memoizes
  // the result) only when count actually changes. It is lazy: it does
  // NOT run until something reads doubleCount() for the first time.
  doubleCount = computed(() => this.count() * 2);

  cartItems = signal<CartItem[]>([
    { name: 'Keyboard', price: 1200, qty: 1 },
  ]);

  // Two more computed signals, both derived from the SAME source signal.
  // Neither recomputes unless cartItems() actually changes.
  itemCount = computed(() => this.cartItems().reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.qty, 0));
  cartTotal = computed(() => this.cartItems().reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0));

  constructor() {
    // effect(): reruns automatically whenever any signal read inside it changes.
    // GOOD USE: talking to something OUTSIDE Angular's reactivity system --
    // here, the browser's document.title API. This is not "app state", so
    // computed() would not make sense for it.
    effect(() => {
      document.title = 'Count: ' + this.count();
    });

    // GOOD USE #2: persistence side effect (localStorage is an external API).
    effect(() => {
      localStorage.setItem('cart', JSON.stringify(this.cartItems()));
      console.log('[effect] cart synced to localStorage ->', this.cartItems());
    });

    // ANTI-PATTERN (commented out on purpose -- do NOT do this):
    //
    // effect(() => {
    //   // Reading cartItems() and WRITING to another signal derived from it.
    //   // This should be computed(), not effect(). Using effect() here:
    //   //  - adds a redundant, hidden reactive hop (harder to trace than computed)
    //   //  - risks an infinite loop if the written signal is ever read back
    //   //    inside this same effect, or inside another effect that feeds back in
    //   //  - runs AFTER change detection instead of being part of the synchronous
    //   //    derivation graph, so consumers can transiently see a stale value
    //   this.itemCountBad.set(this.cartItems().length);
    // });
  }

  increment(): void {
    this.count.update((v) => v + 1);
  }

  decrement(): void {
    this.count.update((v) => v - 1);
  }

  addItem(): void {
    const names = ['Mouse', 'Monitor', 'Cable', 'Webcam'];
    const name = names[Math.floor(Math.random() * names.length)];
    const price = Math.floor(Math.random() * 500) + 100;
    // .update() gives you the previous value and expects a new value back --
    // always return a NEW array/object so Angular's equality check sees a change.
    this.cartItems.update((items) => [...items, { name, price, qty: 1 }]);
  }
}
```

